{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1540 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ccd16278e701aec7d19a1b410b86f747bd8283ca8b7628fc9acba5c9c49b116f" :edb/db-before-t 1539 :edb/db-after-t 1540 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534852 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:05:49.901Z" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203677 1071 "Use the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n![](images/14/14cdf48e441388f15954ecb2ffbe7e56d653abb1dfaa3d95b2abd573e6318cca.svg)\n\n![](images/e6/e60ebf4ac02326cff610f1d4238bb2b8c16062aaa8a50c7cc040631e65d3a5b0.png)" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203677 1071 "Use the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n$0$\n\n![](images/e6/e60ebf4ac02326cff610f1d4238bb2b8c16062aaa8a50c7cc040631e65d3a5b0.png)" 13194139534852 17592186045417 false] [17592186203677 1074 "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $2$ wholes split into sixths.\n\n![](images/6c/6c4328991ef5f89f91b3df5b4d43f8034d58249442700d59c2d842a44c9e8cda.png)\n\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}$ sixths in each group, with $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ remaining.\n\n![](images/e0/e08a28fee137c40615d7261b6282dce8dd0392084a8d801f9cc848364a3ad0f5.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There are $\\color{red}2$ full groups.\n\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ sixths left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{5}{6} = {\\color{red}{2}} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}}.\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203677 1074 "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $2$ wholes split into sixths.![](images/6c/6c4328991ef5f89f91b3df5b4d43f8034d58249442700d59c2d842a44c9e8cda.png)\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}$ sixths in each group, with $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ remaining.![](images/e0/e08a28fee137c40615d7261b6282dce8dd0392084a8d801f9cc848364a3ad0f5.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- There are $\\color{red}2$ full groups.\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ sixths left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}$. So the fractional part of our answer is\n$\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{5}{6} = {\\color{red}{2}} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{5}}\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 false] [17592186203679 1071 "![](images/60/60034ce6b9e7e7e5cd24c8f6f2f3d80a1bbc5d3a8532f3c4ccaeb3d89eaaeb1d.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}.$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203679 1071 "![](images/60/60034ce6b9e7e7e5cd24c8f6f2f3d80a1bbc5d3a8532f3c4ccaeb3d89eaaeb1d.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}$." 13194139534852 17592186045417 false] [17592186203679 1074 "To calculate\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\n, we first need to divide each of the $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}7$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/a3/a373e9e5c6a5dc23240029d2d89fef66f4b9959ffef349f5ea351fffc897d3c8.png)\n\nNext, we break this into groups of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}$:\n\n![](images/1c/1cfe8f5a08ee9464489cacef94b0d94982def065a9ab290acc9ebbec6efc7bf7.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We got $\\fbox{4}$ full groups.\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ pieces left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}$. So the fractional part of our answer is\n$\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = \\fbox{4} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 false] [17592186203679 1074 "To calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}{\\color{blue}7}$, we first need to divide each of the $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}7$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/a3/a373e9e5c6a5dc23240029d2d89fef66f4b9959ffef349f5ea351fffc897d3c8.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we break this into groups of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}$:\n\n![](images/1c/1cfe8f5a08ee9464489cacef94b0d94982def065a9ab290acc9ebbec6efc7bf7.png)\n\n \n\nNote the following: \n\n- We got $\\fbox{4}$ full groups.\n\n- There are $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}$ pieces left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = \\fbox{4} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{2}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{3}}.\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441020 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441024 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441025 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441026 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441029 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441021 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441022 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441023 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441027 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186203968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441028 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186204108 1050 420.0 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186205225 1064 "When we divide a whole number by a non-unit fraction, the answer is often not a whole number.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to solve division problems in these cases using models. The process is similar to cases when the answer is a whole number (we saw this in a previous lesson). The difference now is that we have to deal with a remainder.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's use a model to solve the following division problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe start with a model that represents $\\color{blue}1$ whole:\n\n![](images/bf/bf3da76c75a78c8f2fd64f0ad08a7de339734de12a927d164e7b0b85be5c8bca.png)\n\nWe need to determine how many times\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}3\n$$\n\nfits into $\\color{blue}1$ whole. So, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split our whole into *thirds* (the denominator of our fraction):![](images/bf/bfebfebd8b72bbfe256f4cecadaa0b264be7640117b915aa574a485a0091ab09.png)\n- Next, we break this into groups of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}$:![](images/ac/ac3eaf757cee480d078152c974e98c9c9470cb0bb4f1ac36aefc973c40edfaef.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We have $\\fbox{1}$ full group. This is the whole number part of our answer.\n- We see that there is $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}$ piece left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}$. So, the fractional part of our answer is\n$\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our division problem is as follows:\n\n$$\n1 \\div \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\fbox{1} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534852 17592186045417 false] [17592186205225 1064 "When we divide a whole number by a non-unit fraction, the answer is often not a whole number. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to solve division problems in these cases using models. The process is similar to cases when the answer is a whole number (we saw this in a previous lesson). The difference now is that we have to deal with a remainder. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's use a model to solve the following division problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe start with a model that represents $\\color{blue}1$ whole:\n\n![](images/bf/bf3da76c75a78c8f2fd64f0ad08a7de339734de12a927d164e7b0b85be5c8bca.png)\n\n \n\nWe need to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}3$ fits into $\\color{blue}1$ whole. So, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split our whole into *thirds* (the denominator of our fraction):\n\n![](images/bf/bfebfebd8b72bbfe256f4cecadaa0b264be7640117b915aa574a485a0091ab09.png)\n\n- Next, we break this into groups of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}$:\n\n![](images/ac/ac3eaf757cee480d078152c974e98c9c9470cb0bb4f1ac36aefc973c40edfaef.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We have $\\fbox{1}$ full group. This is the whole number part of our answer.\n\n- We see that there is $\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}$ piece left over out of a possible group of $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}.$ So, the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our division problem is as follows:\n\n$$\n1 \\div \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\fbox{1} \\, \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, Moccasin]{1}}{\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{2}}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440968 1080 "3\\,\\frac{1}{8}" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440968 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440968 1083 #uuid "0011bed5-f6a5-513b-93c2-e64d7d827c64" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440969 1080 "3\\,\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440969 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440969 1083 #uuid "0adb188f-a662-527d-866b-95e2f2aa5ebf" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440970 1080 "2\\,\\frac{3}{8}" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440970 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440970 1083 #uuid "17fc40ef-ae0e-5eb9-a975-4809a02e32f6" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440971 1080 "\\frac{4}{7}" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186440971 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [1759218