9 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441000 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441019 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441008 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441019 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441009 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441019 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441019 1077 "selection" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441019 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441009 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441019 1079 #uuid "ec8469be-de7c-5e03-aec5-58ab0f7fff30" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441013 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441020 1069 "q-237493" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441020 1071 "![](images/4d/4d57e8e8f607d2897a32531acd59ed0ed3f96499d9f2ff6468956be09c6cca65.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{5}{6}=3\\,\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441020 1072 #uuid "4403992c-2eff-5cc7-8e99-ee2d1e59b055" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441020 1074 "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $3$ wholes split into sixths.\n\n![](images/9e/9e4273830a4a7c4f5cc705d6c60ae0ec2f973fd36c0bfb25bafd2024107135b7.png)\n\n- There are $5$ sixths in each group, with $3$ remaining.\n\n![](images/bf/bfa0ff01c63742ffc30dcaeb2b4bd2d6c2f8c97dcfcce9e09707e54751e528aa.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There are $3$ full groups.\n\n- There are $3$ sixths left over out of a possible group of $5.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{5}{6}=3\\,\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441014 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441021 1069 "q-237511" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441021 1071 "![](images/53/530a07a528037b95227f1616d9a74e56874a839e2396a1cb053a417e4407659d.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate the value of $4÷\\frac{5}{6}.$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441021 1072 #uuid "5c17e7c1-7039-5ee7-ba75-14d302d2f636" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441021 1074 "To calculate $4÷\\frac{5}{6}$, we first need to divide each of the $4$ wholes into $6$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/90/90771544aed1e49a45f857d5fe041476a0f8e6130c87466f6853e76468f1fa14.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we break this into groups of $5$:\n\n![](images/23/2343c93c7c92c4510b5f19b2cd3d9e2221da6f69996009ac4e6b669bbcac5278.png)\n\n \n\nNote the following: \n\n- We got $\\boxed{\\text{4}}$ full groups.\n\n- There are $4$ pieces left over out of a possible group of $5.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{5}{6}=\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\,\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441017 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441022 1069 "q-237502" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441022 1071 "![](images/38/38a8f6963fbe538fc67310e583271d8feeef3b86ec9f1e81354f179fe727963f.png)\n\nIf the model above represents $1$ whole, use the model to solve the following division problem. Express your answer as a mixed number in its simplest form.\n\n$1÷\\frac{3}{8}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441022 1072 #uuid "68cff8ab-d96c-583e-af11-a7fb638626de" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441022 1074 "To calculate $1÷\\frac{3}{8}$, we first need to divide the whole into $8$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/e5/e5c3ad39f7ebf8bb6585dfa65054bb6b0eba881f1e9f7404ae5275f5c45f3771.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we break this into groups of $3$:\n\n![](images/db/dbfd34916dd6db9467988b6b72052f9b077e4de67e2b54b9a092d959233ccc85.png)\n\n \n\nNote the following: \n\n- We got $\\boxed{\\text{2}}$ full groups.\n\n- There are $2$ parts left over out of a possible group of $3.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1÷\\frac{3}{8}=\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441011 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441023 1069 "q-237514" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441023 1071 "![](images/05/055cfb55d0e1d78762f7a51655031a982fdc2a9743f11331a239aff31e3b772c.png)\n\nIf the model above represents $4$ wholes, use the model to solve the following division problem. Express your answer as a mixed number in its simplest form.\n\n$4÷\\frac{3}{4}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441023 1072 #uuid "7ff8bb9b-dc96-57ee-9dbf-d8dad14fd153" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441023 1074 "To calculate $4÷\\frac{3}{4}$, we first need to divide each of the $4$ wholes into $4$ equal parts.\n\n![](images/13/137a7924f2a2f463a014b3d6e289cc386b7981856febb02f5a1f7f7454e8ea1f.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we break this into groups of $3$:\n\n![](images/a5/a569b682856cef52674f2fd517577c04a543a3ba385c6d904ec2955c9f792f53.png)\n\n \n\nNote the following: \n\n- We got $\\boxed{\\text{5}}$ full groups.\n\n- There is $1$ part left over out of a possible group of $3.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\boxed{\\text{5}}\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441012 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441024 1069 "q-237497" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441024 1071 "![](images/82/8236faf3fbf8835d70f790685b2411a86d7856049f3be