group.\n\n- There is $1$ quarter left over out of a possible group of $3.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1÷\\frac{3}{4}=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441018 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441026 1069 "q-237496" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441026 1071 "![](images/90/90d15521c8aa8ef53e0659debffdb85010332cd8ba653ca52b6e36a40a05b57a.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{7}{8}=4\\,\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441026 1072 #uuid "b01871bf-cff8-540f-8321-0bc0568904bd" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441026 1074 "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $4$ wholes split into eighths.\n\n![](images/a2/a216109fd3b33a037b060dc832e208e2d2162d5a13b405a2a06b39e5f6656668.png)\n\n- There are $7$ eighths in each group, with $4$ remaining.\n\n![](images/7a/7a3a4edb355d0c6fd7138dcfe56c420a4a52faeff12d12437d4b152294e6283e.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There are $4$ full groups.\n\n- There are $4$ eighths left over out of a possible group of $7.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{7}{8}=4\\,\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441015 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441027 1069 "q-61376" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441027 1071 "![](images/62/62bc017c2a920b6e1014fe652ce847d76b062bf2290e2916a99b830e8e10db45.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate the value of $2÷\\frac{3}{5}.$" 13194139534852 17592186045417 true] [17592186441027 1072 #uuid "b3177db0-b185-5ef2-96e9-